// can_ping.csp -- round trip between two Feather RP2040 CAN boards, with the
// numbers that actually tell you something about the bus.
//
// Same file on both boards; swap the two ids on the second one:
//   board A: out 0x200, in 0x201        board B: out 0x201, in 0x200
//
// WHAT IT MEASURES. Each board stamps its own send counter into every frame.
// The receiver counts what it actually got. The difference is what went
// missing -- one subtraction, no bookkeeping:
//
//     Lost = PeerSeq - (Rcv + 1)
//
// A blink tells you the bus is alive. A gap counter tells you whether it is
// any good, which is the question a first bring-up is really asking.

#buffer Tx:8 out can 0x200
#buffer Rx:8 in  can 0x201

#field TxSeq:16  unsigned Tx[0..15]
#field TxEcho:16 unsigned Tx[16..31]   // the last sequence we heard back
#field RxSeq:16  unsigned Rx[0..15]
#field RxEcho:16 unsigned Rx[16..31]

#digital Led out 13

#timer Beat 500 = 1                    // send rate; patch Beat.period live
#timer Log  1000 = 1                   // UART heartbeat, deliberately slower

#variable Seq  = 0                     // frames we have sent
#variable Rcv  = 0                     // frames we have received
#variable Lost = 0                     // what the peer sent and we never saw
#variable Trip = 0                     // our seq minus the one echoed back
#variable Got:1 unsigned = 0

Beat = 1 ? timeout(Beat)
Log  = 1 ? timeout(Log)

// --- send ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Assigning a field is the send. Both fields are written in the same cycle so
// the frame goes out once, not twice.
Seq = Seq + 1 ? timeout(Beat)
TxSeq = Seq, TxEcho = RxSeq ? timeout(Beat)

// --- receive ---------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE-CYCLE TRAP, and the only subtle thing in this file. `.rx` fires while
// the new payload is still in the shadow copy, so reading RxSeq in the same
// cycle gives the PREVIOUS frame. Route the trigger through a variable: Got is
// set on the .rx cycle and read on the next one, by which time the payload has
// committed.
Got = 1 ? Rx.rx
Got = 0 ? Got

Rcv  = Rcv + 1 ? Got
// Rcv + 1, not Rcv: both rules read the value committed last cycle, so at this
// instant Rcv has not yet counted the frame we are looking at. Without the +1
// every board reports one lost frame forever.
Lost = RxSeq - (Rcv + 1) ? Got         // peer's count minus ours
Trip = Seq - RxEcho ? Got              // how far behind our echo is
Led  = !Led ? Got

// --- report ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Once a second, not once a frame: printing per frame chokes the UART and
// changes the timing you are trying to measure.
println("seq=", Seq, " rcv=", Rcv) ? timeout(Log)
println("  lost=", Lost, " trip=", Trip) ? timeout(Log)

// --- rate ramp (leave OFF for the first run) --------------------------------
// Sweeps the send period between fast and slow so a marginal bus shows up as a
// stumble instead of a flat pass. Enable once the fixed rate is clean --
// otherwise a failure has two candidate causes and you learn nothing.
//
// #timer Ramp 2000 = 1
// #variable Down:1 unsigned = 1
// Ramp = 1 ? timeout(Ramp)
// Beat.period = Beat.period - 20 ? timeout(Ramp) && Down && Beat.period > 40
// Down = 0 ? timeout(Ramp) && Down && Beat.period <= 40
// Beat.period = Beat.period + 20 ? timeout(Ramp) && !Down && Beat.period < 500
// Down = 1 ? timeout(Ramp) && !Down && Beat.period >= 500
